["Separate access help from account authority","Approved support may include identifying the learner, confirming enrollment status, resending a standard access link, and guiding the learner through documented steps. Email changes, merged accounts, exports, impersonation, refunds, and broad administrative permissions need stronger controls. Use individual accounts with the narrowest platform role. Test recovery and revocation before the course opens. A learner's urgent deadline does not justify asking for a password or bypassing identity checks."],
["Create an accessibility-request route","Publish a clear contact path and train the assistant to preserve the learner's request, acknowledge it without making unsupported promises, and send it promptly to the responsible owner. Do not ask the assistant to judge whether someone is entitled to an accommodation or to solicit unnecessary medical details. The ADA web guidance offers general digital-accessibility context; the course operator should use qualified advice for its obligations. Track request status in a restricted record and keep it out of public community channels."],
["Moderate from rules, not personal instinct","Define community rules, low-risk actions, warning language, removal authority, urgent safety escalation, appeal ownership, and evidence retention. Distinguish disagreement from harassment, spam from enthusiastic sharing, and a support complaint from a moderation issue. The assistant can apply clearly documented controls and flag ambiguous cases. Avoid performance targets based only on removed posts, which reward over-moderation. Sample decisions across moderators and examine consistency, response quality, and unnecessary exposure of learner information."],
